One of the most abundant minerals in the Earth’s crust, quartz is essential for manufacturing core components of smartphones and computers. Most of the world’s supply comes from a single mining district in the Appalachian Mountains of the southeast United States, which has become an unlikely linchpin of the global tech economy.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5168e916-7c72-11f1-9145-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Spruce-Pine-Pegmatite.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5168e916-7c72-11f1-9145-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Spruce-Pine-Pegmatite.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">984c269a-81c7-11f1-95a5-1f0843f75d2d</guid><pubDate>Sat, 18 Jul 2026 16:02:25 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/5168e916-7c72-11f1-9145-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/Spruce-Pine-Pegmatite.png">© Marguliesgm, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>International</category><title>US-Iran fighting escalates with infrastructure targeted</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/international/20260718-us-iran-fighting-escalates-with-infrastructure-targeted</link><description>
Iran on Saturday struck infrastructure in the Gulf and threatened a "full-scale offensive" in retaliation for the seventh consecutive night of US strikes on the Islamic republic.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3f5fe1d8-8227-11f1-96d1-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/64be5ebe968fbf6d8c93cbaf54b722cd4931a830.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3f5fe1d8-8227-11f1-96d1-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/64be5ebe968fbf6d8c93cbaf54b722cd4931a830.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e8ca91d0-82a1-11f1-b21d-b7dd12542a41</guid><pubDate>Sat, 18 Jul 2026 12:12:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3f5fe1d8-8227-11f1-96d1-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/64be5ebe968fbf6d8c93cbaf54b722cd4931a830.jpg">© - / US MARINE CORPS/AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Zimbabwe’s gold rush adds fuel to a deadly surge in malaria</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260718-zimbabwe-s-gold-rush-adds-fuel-to-a-deadly-surge-in-malaria</link><description>
In Zimbabwe, open pits left by informal gold miners and filled with stagnant water create breeding grounds for mosquitoes. Health experts say mining is worsening a surge in malaria infections, already spiralling due to funding cuts and climate change.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a7e4c1ee-81da-11f1-822a-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/A-miner-panning-for-gold-along-Mutare-River-in-Mutasa-Manicaland-Province-Farai-Shawn-Matiashe-HEIC.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a7e4c1ee-81da-11f1-822a-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/A-miner-panning-for-gold-along-Mutare-River-in-Mutasa-Manicaland-Province-Farai-Shawn-Matiashe-HEIC.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fac7f3e0-81c1-11f1-b1ce-0f8c4be464d3</guid><pubDate>Sat, 18 Jul 2026 14:28:19 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a7e4c1ee-81da-11f1-822a-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/A-miner-panning-for-gold-along-Mutare-River-in-Mutasa-Manicaland-Province-Farai-Shawn-Matiashe-HEIC.jpg">© Farai Shawn Matiashe</source><dc:creator>Farai Shawn Matiashe</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Malawi rolls out injectable HIV prevention drug amid hope and caution</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260718-malawi-rolls-out-injectable-hiv-prevention-drug-amid-hope-and-caution</link><description>
Patrick Chakwana, 50, has lived with HIV since 1999, when there was no effective treatment and the message to those diagnosed was blunt: "Aids kills". Today, as Malawi becomes one of the latest African countries to roll out lenacapavir, a twice-yearly injectable drug that prevents HIV infection, Chakwana's reaction is one of hope, tempered by caution.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/1f017a70-81f3-11f1-b566-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Thunga-photo-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/1f017a70-81f3-11f1-b566-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Thunga-photo-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6ccb95e8-81f2-11f1-8d2c-a5cf1b445228</guid><pubDate>Sat, 18 Jul 2026 10:35:05 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/1f017a70-81f3-11f1-b566-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Thunga-photo-1.jpg">© RFI/ Charles Pensulo</source><dc:creator>Charles Pensulo in Malawi</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>France-Morocco rapprochement overshadowed by fresh Pegasus spying claims</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260718-france-morocco-rapprochement-overshadowed-by-fresh-pegasus-spying-claims</link><description>
As France seeks to rebuild ties with Morocco after years of diplomatic tensions, a new investigation claims Moroccan intelligence targeted senior French officials with Israeli-made Pegasus spyware. 
